{"packet_id":"D067-04","doc_id":"D067","jurisdiction":"NJ","category":"just_cause_eviction","lifecycle":"enacted","title":"NJ Eviction Law criminal-activity eviction grounds","requirement":"A landlord may evict a tenant convicted of, or a juvenile adjudicated delinquent for, assault or threats against the landlord, the landlord's family or employees, theft of property, or human trafficking, or found civilly liable for certain on-premises criminal activity.","key_value":null,"coverage_conditions":"Residential rental tenants in New Jersey, including two- and three-family dwellings; certain owner-occupied small dwellings are handled under a separate section.","applicability":{"built_on_or_before":null,"built_after":null,"date_basis":null,"min_units":null,"max_units":null,"owner_dependent":false,"other":null},"exemptions":null,"penalty":null,"effective_date":null,"citation":"N.J.S.A. 2A:18-61.1","quoted_span":"The landlord may file for eviction, if the tenant has been convicted of or pleaded guilty to, or if a juvenile has been found to be delinquent by the Court due to an offense involving theft of property from the landlord or from tenants residing in the same building or complex.","interaction":null,"confidence":0.75,"conflict_flag":false,"conflict_note":null}
{"packet_id":"D067-04","doc_id":"D067","jurisdiction":"NJ","category":"just_cause_eviction","lifecycle":"enacted","title":"Eviction from owner-occupied two- and three-family dwellings","requirement":"Tenants of a landlord-occupied two- or three-family dwelling may be evicted only by court order for specified causes, such as nonpayment, disorderly conduct, damage, or rule and lease violations; three months' notice applies to at-will and year-to-year tenancies and one month to month-to-month.","key_value":"3-month notice to quit; 1-month for month-to-month","coverage_conditions":"Landlord-occupied two- and three-family dwellings; eviction of tenants in these dwellings only by court order on the listed causes.","applicability":{"built_on_or_before":null,"built_after":null,"date_basis":null,"min_units":2,"max_units":3,"owner_dependent":true,"other":null},"exemptions":null,"penalty":null,"effective_date":null,"citation":"N.J.S.A. 2A:18-61.2","quoted_span":"Tenants of landlord-occupied two- and three-family dwellings can be removed only when a court issues an order for eviction.","interaction":null,"confidence":0.75,"conflict_flag":false,"conflict_note":null}
{"packet_id":"D067-04","doc_id":"D067","jurisdiction":"NJ","category":"just_cause_eviction","lifecycle":"enacted","title":"Rooming and boarding house eviction requirements","requirement":"Operators of rooming and boarding houses must follow the good-cause and notice requirements of the Eviction Law when evicting residents and must give at least three working days' notice to the County Welfare Board before starting an eviction action.","key_value":"3 working days' notice to County Welfare Board","coverage_conditions":"Rooming and boarding houses: buildings with at least two living units occupied by unrelated persons without private kitchens and bathrooms, subject to statutory exceptions.","applicability":{"built_on_or_before":null,"built_after":null,"date_basis":null,"min_units":2,"max_units":null,"owner_dependent":false,"other":"Rooming and boarding houses"},"exemptions":"Hotels with more than 85 percent temporary occupancy by people with homes elsewhere, school and college dormitories, buildings housing only college students, and certain residences for the disabled.","penalty":null,"effective_date":null,"citation":"N.J.A.C. 5:27-3.3(c)","quoted_span":"The Regulations Governing Rooming and Boarding Houses, which are enforced by the Bureau of Rooming and Boarding House Standards of the Department of Community Affairs, require owners of rooming and boarding houses to follow the good causes and notice requirements of the Eviction Law when evicting residents, except if otherwise ordered by the Bureau (N.J.A.C. 5:27-3.3(c)).","interaction":null,"confidence":0.75,"conflict_flag":false,"conflict_note":null}
{"packet_id":"D067-04","doc_id":"D067","jurisdiction":"NJ","category":"just_cause_eviction","lifecycle":"enacted","title":"Penalties for Eviction Law violations","requirement":"A landlord who gave notice to personally occupy a unit must occupy it at least six months; otherwise the landlord owes three times the former tenant's damages plus attorney fees. A demolition or retirement notice bars residential use for five years.","key_value":"6 months' owner occupancy; 5-year residential-use bar","coverage_conditions":"Dwelling units vacated after an owner-occupancy, board-up, demolition, or permanent retirement eviction notice.","applicability":{"built_on_or_before":null,"built_after":null,"date_basis":null,"min_units":null,"max_units":null,"owner_dependent":true,"other":null},"exemptions":null,"penalty":"Three times the damages suffered plus attorney fees and costs; civil penalty up to $10,000 for each violation of the five-year residential-use bar.","effective_date":null,"citation":"N.J.S.A. 2A:18-61.6","quoted_span":"If instead the landlord permits personal occupancy of the unit by another tenant or registration of conversion of the property to a condominium or cooperative, the landlord is liable to the former tenant for three (3) times the damages suffered plus attorney fees and costs (N.J.S.A. 2A:18-61.6(a)).","interaction":null,"confidence":0.75,"conflict_flag":false,"conflict_note":null}
{"packet_id":"D067-04","doc_id":"D067","jurisdiction":"NJ","category":"just_cause_eviction","lifecycle":"enacted","title":"Reprisal against tenants exercising civil rights","requirement":"A landlord may not evict, substantially alter a lease, or refuse to renew in retaliation for a tenant enforcing lease or legal rights, making a good-faith health or safety complaint, or organizing.","key_value":null,"coverage_conditions":"All rental properties used for dwelling purposes, including mobile homes.","applicability":{"built_on_or_before":null,"built_after":null,"date_basis":null,"min_units":null,"max_units":3,"owner_dependent":true,"other":null},"exemptions":"Owner-occupied two- or three-family dwellings.","penalty":"Tenant may sue the landlord for damages in a civil action.","effective_date":null,"citation":"N.J.S.A. 2A:42-10.10","quoted_span":"A landlord cannot take reprisal action against a tenant by eviction, substantial alteration of a lease or its terms, or refusal to renew a lease when a tenant exercises certain civil rights (N.J.S.A. 2A:42-10.10).","interaction":null,"confidence":0.75,"conflict_flag":false,"conflict_note":null}
{"packet_id":"D067-04","doc_id":"D067","jurisdiction":"NJ","category":"just_cause_eviction","lifecycle":"enacted","title":"Tenant protections in foreclosure","requirement":"Foreclosure alone is not grounds for eviction; a buyer who will personally occupy the foreclosed property as a primary residence must give the tenant a 90-day notice to quit. New owners must notify tenants of the ownership change.","key_value":"90-day notice to quit","coverage_conditions":"Residential rental tenants in properties that have been foreclosed and sold to a new owner.","applicability":{"built_on_or_before":null,"built_after":null,"date_basis":null,"min_units":null,"max_units":null,"owner_dependent":false,"other":null},"exemptions":null,"penalty":null,"effective_date":null,"citation":"N.J.S.A. 2A:50-69 et seq.","quoted_span":"Before a tenant can be evicted due to foreclosure, the landlord must provide the tenant with a 90 -day notice to qui t when the foreclosed property has been purchased by a buyer who wants to personally occupy it as his or her primary residence.","interaction":"Federal foreclosure law does not preempt State or local law that provides longer time periods or other additional protections for tenants.","confidence":0.75,"conflict_flag":false,"conflict_note":null}
{"packet_id":"D067-04","doc_id":"D067","jurisdiction":"NJ","category":"just_cause_eviction","lifecycle":"enacted","title":"Senior Citizens and Disabled Protected Tenancy Act","requirement":"Senior citizens aged 62 or older, permanently disabled tenants, and certain disabled veterans who lived in a building converted to condominium, cooperative, or fee simple ownership at least one year before the conversion recording may be protected from eviction for 40 years if income-qualified.","key_value":"40 years' protection","coverage_conditions":"Tenants in buildings converted to condominium, cooperative, or fee simple ownership who meet the age, disability, or veteran status and residency requirements and income limits.","applicability":{"built_on_or_before":null,"built_after":null,"date_basis":null,"min_units":null,"max_units":null,"owner_dependent":false,"other":"Buildings converted or being converted to condominium, cooperative, or fee simple ownership"},"exemptions":null,"penalty":null,"effective_date":null,"citation":"N.J.S.A. 2A:18-61.22 to -61.39","quoted_span":"Tenants may be protected from eviction for 40 years if their family income is not more than three (3) times the average per person income in their county or $50,000.00, whichever is greater (N.J.S.A. 2A:18-61.28).","interaction":null,"confidence":0.75,"conflict_flag":false,"conflict_note":null}
{"packet_id":"D067-04","doc_id":"D067","jurisdiction":"NJ","category":"just_cause_eviction","lifecycle":"enacted","title":"Tenant Protection Act of 1992","requirement":"The Tenant Protection Act of 1992 extends protected-tenancy protections to qualified tenants in qualified counties, currently only Hudson County, who were not eligible as senior citizens or disabled persons under the 1981 Act.","key_value":null,"coverage_conditions":"Qualified tenants in qualified counties, currently only Hudson County, in buildings converted or being converted to condominium, cooperative, or fee simple ownership.","applicability":{"built_on_or_before":null,"built_after":null,"date_basis":null,"min_units":null,"max_units":null,"owner_dependent":false,"other":"Qualified counties; currently only Hudson County"},"exemptions":null,"penalty":null,"effective_date":null,"citation":"N.J.S.A. 2A:18-61.40 to -61.59","quoted_span":"The Tenant Protection Law of 1992 amendment extends protections to qualified tenants in qualified counties in buildings converted or being converted who were not eligible for Protected Tenancy as either Senior Citizens or Disabled Persons under the “Senior Citizens and Disabled Protected Tenancy Act of 1981” (N.J.S.A. 2A:18-61.40 to -61.59; N.J.A.C. 5:24-3.1 to -3.4).","interaction":null,"confidence":0.7,"conflict_flag":false,"conflict_note":null}
{"packet_id":"D067-04","doc_id":"D067","jurisdiction":"NJ","category":"rent_increase_limits","lifecycle":"enacted","title":"Prohibited rent increases (rent control and unconscionable increases)","requirement":"An increase in rent above any applicable rent control or rent leveling ordinance, or, where the property is not subject to rent control, an unreasonable or unconscionable rent increase, is listed as prohibited conduct.","key_value":"Increase above rent control; unreasonable or unconscionable increase","coverage_conditions":"Rental housing subject to a municipal rent control or rent leveling ordinance, and rental housing not subject to rent control.","applicability":{"built_on_or_before":null,"built_after":null,"date_basis":null,"min_units":null,"max_units":null,"owner_dependent":false,"other":"Rent-controlled and non-rent-controlled rental housing"},"exemptions":null,"penalty":null,"effective_date":null,"citation":null,"quoted_span":"An increase in rent in excess of any rent control or rent leveling ordinance, or if the property is not subject to rent control, an unreasonable or unconscionable rent increase.","interaction":null,"confidence":0.4,"conflict_flag":false,"conflict_note":null}
